```js
function throttle(fn, wait) {
  let last = 0;
  let timer = null;

  return function (...args) {
    const remaining = wait - (Date.now() - last);

    if (remaining <= 0) {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      timer = null;
      last = Date.now();
      fn.apply(this, args);
    } else if (!timer) {
      // Trailing call so the final scroll position isn't missed
      timer = setTimeout(() => {
        last = Date.now();
        timer = null;
        fn.apply(this, args);
      }, remaining);
    }
  };
}

function onScroll() {
  console.log('scrollY:', window.scrollY);
}

const throttledScroll = throttle(onScroll, 200);

window.addEventListener('scroll', throttledScroll, { passive: true });

// Later, to clean up:
// window.removeEventListener('scroll', throttledScroll);
```

The handler fires immediately on the first scroll event, then at most once per 200 ms while scrolling continues. One trailing call runs after scrolling stops, so the handler always sees the final scroll position.

Keep the reference to `throttledScroll`, since `removeEventListener` needs the same function you added. `{ passive: true }` tells the browser the handler won't call `preventDefault()`, which keeps scrolling smooth.

If you already use Lodash, `_.throttle(onScroll, 200)` does the same thing.
